<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colombia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Colombia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colombia is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, with insular regions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, bordered by Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. Indigenous peoples lived there from at least 12,000 BCE. The Spanish landed in La Guajira in 1499 and by the mid-16th century had colonised much of the territory, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Independence was declared in 1810, and after a Spanish reconquest it was secured in 1819, when the Gran Colombia period began. The state became the Granadine Confederation (1858), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Colombia (1863) and a centralised republic in 1886. Panama seceded in 1903, which gave Colombia its present borders.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its official name was simply the Republic of <b>Colombia</b>; historians later coined ‘Gran Colombia’ to distinguish it from the present-day country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…less than a century the Incas united a larger area and population than any other state of the pre-Columbian Americas, stretching from southern <b>Colombia</b> to central Chile, and integrated coastal, highland and forest peoples into one administrative system without money, markets on a large scale or alphabetic writing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muisca heartland became the core of the New Kingdom of Granada: Santa Fe de Bogotá, founded in 1538 on the savanna of the zipas, became the seat of a royal audiencia created in 1549, and later the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Granada and of <b>Colombia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Andrés Bello, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its Civil Code of 1855, which was later adopted by Ecuador and <b>Colombia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Rafael Núñez, the president behind the constitution of 1886, also wrote the words of <b>Colombia</b>’s national anthem, “¡Oh gloria inmarcesible!”, made official in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory was then incorporated into Simón Bolívar’s Gran <b>Colombia</b> as its Southern District.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
